{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2703 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d96b11665752c9edc33173f8f8ff58de291e09989242538efe596afa47b1cb4c" :edb/db-before-t 2702 :edb/db-after-t 2703 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536015 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:58:36.680Z" 13194139536015 true] [17592186144407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379469 13194139536015 true] [17592186144407 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379469 13194139536015 true] [17592186147305 1100 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y(t)\\right\\}$ for $s\\geq\\sqrt5$ if\n$y'' - 5y = 4e^{-3t}, \\quad y(0)=2, \\quad y'(0)=-1$." 13194139536015 false] [17592186147305 1100 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y(t)\\right\\}$ for $s\\geq\\sqrt5$ if \n\n$$\ny'' - 5y = 4e^{-3t}, \\quad y(0)=2, \\quad y'(0)=-1.\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186147305 1145 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″} - 5y} &= L{4e^{-3t}} \\\\\nL{y^{″}} - 5L{y} &= 4L{e^{-3t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}=s^2\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y\\right\\}-sy(0)-y'(0)=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}=s^2Y(s)-2s+1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-3t}\\right\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s+3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″}} - 5L{y} &= 4L{e^{-3t}} \\\\\n(s^{2}Y(s) - 2s + 1) - 5Y(s) &= \\frac{4}{s + 3} \\\\\n(s^{2} - 5)Y(s) &= \\frac{4}{s + 3} + 2s - 1 \\\\\n(s^{2} - 5)Y(s) &= (4 + (2s - 1)(s + 3))/(s + 3) \\\\\n(s^{2} - 5)Y(s) &= \\frac{2s^{2} + 5s + 1}{s + 3} \\\\\nY(s) &= (2s^{2} + 5s + 1)/((s + 3)(s^{2} - 5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536015 false] [17592186147305 1145 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}-5y} & =L{4{e}^{-3t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}-5L{y} & =4L{{e}^{-3t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}=s^2\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y\\right\\}-sy(0)-y'(0)=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}=s^2Y(s)-2s+1.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-3t}\\right\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s+3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}-5L{y} & =4L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-2s+1)-5Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{s+3} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-5)Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{s+3}+2s-1 \\\\ ({s}^{2}-5)Y(s) & =\\frac{4+(2s-1)(s+3)}{s+3} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-5)Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+5s+1}{s+3} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+5s+1}{(s+3)({s}^{2}-5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186147306 1100 "Find $y(t)$ if the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +  6y = 2\\sin {2t}, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad y'(0) = 2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ns^2Y(s) - 2 +6 Y(s)  =  2\\left(\\dfrac{2}{s^2 + 4}\\right).\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186147306 1100 "Find $y(t)$ if the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n$y'' + 6y = 2\\sin {2t}, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad y'(0) = 2$\n\nis given by\n\n$s^2Y(s) - 2 +6 Y(s) = 2\\left(\\dfrac{2}{s^2 + 4}\\right)$." 13194139536015 false] [17592186147306 1145 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns^{2}Y(s) - 2 + 6Y(s) &= 2(\\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}), \\\\\n(s^{2} + 6)Y(s) &= 2 + \\frac{4}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s^{2} + 6)Y(s) &= \\frac{2s^{2} + 12}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s^{2} + 6)Y(s) &= (2(s^{2} + 6))/(s^{2} + 4) \\\\\nY(s) &= \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{\\sin 2t} = \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{Y(s)} &= L^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}} \\\\\ny(t) &= \\sin 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\sin {2t}\n$$\n\nis the solution of the original differential equation." 13194139536015 false] [17592186147306 1145 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-2+6Y(s) & =2(\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}), \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+12}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+6)}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{Y(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y(t) =  \\sin {2t}$ is the solution of the original differential equation." 13194139536015 true] [17592186147455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379462 13194139536015 true] [17592186147455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379484 13194139536015 true] [17592186147455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379491 13194139536015 true] [17592186147455 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379498 13194139536015 true] [17592186147456 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379470 13194139536015 true] [17592186147456 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379474 13194139536015 true] [17592186147456 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379478 13194139536015 true] [17592186147456 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379481 13194139536015 true] [17592186147736 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536015 true] [17592186147736 1100 "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-1-Y(s)=-2(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536015 true] [17592186147736 1100 "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n$y^{″}(t) - y(t) =-2\\sin t, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 1$\n\nis given by\n\n$s^{2}Y(s) - 1 - Y(s) =-2(\\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1})$.\n\nThe solution is $y(t) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536015 false] [17592186147736 1145 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-1-Y(s) & =-2(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =1-\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186148100 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536015 true] [17592186148100 1100 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ for $s \\ge \\sqrt{2}$ if\n\n$y^{″} - 2y = t^{2}, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 2$." 13194139536015 false] [17592186148100 1100 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2y={t}^{2},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186148100 1145 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}-2y} & =L{{t}^{2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}-2L{y} & =L{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-2.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}-2L{y} & =L{{t}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-2)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-2)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{3}+1)}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{3}+1)}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186379462 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379463 13194139536015 true] [17592186379462 1089 "q-102398" 13194139536015 true] [17592186379462 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536015 true] [17592186379462 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y={e}^{-t},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186379462 1113 #uuid "176f4b0f-78af-5c61-b230-6d198c1455ef" 13194139536015 true] [17592186379462 1145 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}-4y} & =L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}-4L{y} & =L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-1.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}-4L{y} & =L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-1)-4Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}+1 \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{s+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{(s+1)({s}^{2}-4)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{(s+1)(s-2)(s+2)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536015 true] [17592186379463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379464 13194139536015 true] [17592186379463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379465 13194139536015 true] [17592186379463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379466 13194139536015 true] [17592186379463 100